<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.80s">• Right for skewed data</text>
<text x="386.0" y="124" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.15s">• Minimizes absolute error</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. il07-math-stats-D1 — </strong>The mean listens to every data point including outliers; the median listens only to the middle. For skewed data such as salaries or latencies, the median describes the typical case honestly.</figcaption></figure>

Bayes: P(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+). Worked example: 1% of requests are malicious (0.01); the detector catches 99% of malicious and flags 5% of benign. P(+) = 0.99×0.01 + 0.05×0.99 = **0.0594**. So P(malicious | flagged) = 0.0099/0.0594 = **0.167** — only about 1 in 6 flags is real. This is the base-rate effect: with rare events, even good tests produce mostly false positives. Every alert-fatigue argument in operations is Bayes in disguise.

