<text x="606.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">act on odds</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. il07-math-stats-D2 — </strong>Bayes&#x27; theorem updates a prior belief with observed evidence to produce a posterior. Ignoring the base rate is why a 99%-accurate test can still be wrong most of the time on a rare condition.</figcaption></figure>

P(X=k) = C(n,k)·p^k·(1−p)^(n−k). Worked example: 10 requests, each fails independently with p=0.5 (a coin). P(exactly 3 fail) = C(10,3)×0.5^3×0.5^7 = 120×0.5^10 = **0.1172**. Sanity: probabilities over k=0..10 sum to 1.000000 = 1 ✓. Use the binomial for 'k of n independent trials' questions — canary failures, bit errors, A/B conversions.

