<text x="170.0" y="82" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.05s">p50 = typical user experience</text>
<text x="170.0" y="106" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.40s">p99 = worst 1% experience</text>
<text x="170.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">SLOs are written in percentiles</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. il07-math-stats-D4 — </strong>Averages hide the users who suffer most: a 50 ms mean can conceal a 5 s tail. Percentiles like p99 pin the tail down, which is why SLOs are always stated as &quot;p99 &lt; X ms&quot;.</figcaption></figure>

Worked example: u=[1, 2, 3], v=[4, -1, 2]. Dot product u·v = 1×4 + 2×(−1) + 3×2 = 4 − 2 + 6 = **8**. Norm ||u|| = √(1+4+9) = √14 ≈ **3.7417**. Cosine similarity = u·v/(||u||·||v||) = 8/(√14×√21) ≈ **0.4666**. Dot products measure alignment: positive = similar direction, zero = orthogonal, negative = opposing. Embeddings, search ranking, and attention scores are all dot products at scale.

